ML_PAGE("l5-notes", "<header class=\"page-header theme-l5\"><span class=\"eyebrow\">CHAPTER 5 · COMPLETE REFERENCE</span><h2>Full Intuitive Explanation</h2><p>The complete Chapter 5 narrative, preserved in full. Use the guided lessons for a first pass; open this when a concept still isn't clicking.</p></header><div class=\"action-row\"><button class=\"primary-btn bg-l5\" data-action=\"print\">Print this page</button><button class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l5-subjective\">Go to subjective prep</button><button class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l5-prep\">Go to MCQ prep</button></div><details class=\"topic-index toc-panel theme-l5\" id=\"l5n-toc\"><summary><strong>Reading order</strong><span class=\"toc-count\">44 sections</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><input type=\"search\" class=\"toc-search\" placeholder=\"Filter sections...\" aria-label=\"Filter sections\"><ul class=\"toc-list\"><li data-full=\"1. What is a portfolio, economically?\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5n-s1\" title=\"1. What is a portfolio, economically?\"><span class=\"toc-no\">1</span>What is a portfolio, economically</button></li><li data-full=\"2. The ultimate goal: an efficient portfolio\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5n-s2\" title=\"2. The ultimate goal: an efficient portfolio\"><span class=\"toc-no\">2</span>The goal: an efficient portfolio</button></li><li data-full=\"3. Portfolio return: surprisingly simple\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5n-s3\" title=\"3. Portfolio return: surprisingly simple\"><span class=\"toc-no\">3</span>Portfolio return</button></li><li data-full=\"4. Portfolio risk: why we cannot just average standard deviations\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5n-s4\" title=\"4. Portfolio risk: why we cannot just average standard deviations\"><span class=\"toc-no\">4</span>Portfolio risk is not an average</button></li><li data-full=\"5. Correlation: why diversification actually works\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5n-s5\" title=\"5. Correlation: why diversification actually works\"><span class=\"toc-no\">5</span>Correlation</button></li><li data-full=\"6. Why negative correlation can reduce portfolio risk\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5n-s6\" title=\"6. Why negative correlation can reduce portfolio risk\"><span class=\"toc-no\">6</span>Negative correlation reduces risk</button></li><li data-full=\"7. The mathematical formula for portfolio risk\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5n-s7\" title=\"7. The mathematical formula for portfolio risk\"><span class=\"toc-no\">7</span>The two-asset σp formula</button></li><li data-full=\"8. Worked example: same assets, different correlations\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5n-s8\" title=\"8. Worked example: same assets, different correlations\"><span class=\"toc-no\">8</span>Worked example: changing ρ</button></li><li data-full=\"9. The chapter&#x27;s X, Y and Z example: an especially important table\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5n-s9\" title=\"9. The chapter&#x27;s X, Y and Z example: an especially important table\"><span class=\"toc-no\">9</span>The X, Y and Z example</button></li><li data-full=\"10. Why portfolio risk is not the weighted average of asset risks\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5n-s10\" title=\"10. Why portfolio risk is not the weighted average of asset risks\"><span class=\"toc-no\">10</span>IBM and Celgene</button></li><li data-full=\"11. International diversification: a different source of correlation benefits\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5n-s11\" title=\"11. International diversification: a different source of correlation benefits\"><span class=\"toc-no\">11</span>International diversification</button></li><li data-full=\"12. Systematic versus unsystematic risk\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5n-s12\" title=\"12. Systematic versus unsystematic risk\"><span class=\"toc-no\">12</span>Systematic versus unsystematic risk</button></li><li data-full=\"13. Why diversification cannot remove all market risk\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5n-s13\" title=\"13. Why diversification cannot remove all market risk\"><span class=\"toc-no\">13</span>Why some risk always remains</button></li><li data-full=\"14. Beta: measuring sensitivity to market movements\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5n-s14\" title=\"14. Beta: measuring sensitivity to market movements\"><span class=\"toc-no\">14</span>Beta</button></li><li data-full=\"15. Beta is not the same thing as standard deviation\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5n-s15\" title=\"15. Beta is not the same thing as standard deviation\"><span class=\"toc-no\">15</span>Beta vs standard deviation</button></li><li data-full=\"16. Where beta comes from mathematically\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5n-s16\" title=\"16. Where beta comes from mathematically\"><span class=\"toc-no\">16</span>Where beta comes from mathematically</button></li><li data-full=\"17. Interpreting beta correctly\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5n-s17\" title=\"17. Interpreting beta correctly\"><span class=\"toc-no\">17</span>Interpreting beta correctly</button></li><li data-full=\"18. Why only systematic risk is rewarded under CAPM\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5n-s18\" title=\"18. Why only systematic risk is rewarded under CAPM\"><span class=\"toc-no\">18</span>Only systematic risk is rewarded</button></li><li data-full=\"19. The Capital Asset Pricing Model (CAPM)\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5n-s19\" title=\"19. The Capital Asset Pricing Model (CAPM)\"><span class=\"toc-no\">19</span>The Capital Asset Pricing Model (CAPM)</button></li><li data-full=\"20. Deriving the economic logic of CAPM\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5n-s20\" title=\"20. Deriving the economic logic of CAPM\"><span class=\"toc-no\">20</span>The logic of CAPM</button></li><li data-full=\"21. What happens if beta is zero or negative?\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5n-s21\" title=\"21. What happens if beta is zero or negative?\"><span class=\"toc-no\">21</span>Zero and negative beta</button></li><li data-full=\"22. The Security Market Line (SML)\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5n-s22\" title=\"22. The Security Market Line (SML)\"><span class=\"toc-no\">22</span>The Security Market Line (SML)</button></li><li data-full=\"23. Above, on or below the Security Market Line\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5n-s23\" title=\"23. Above, on or below the Security Market Line\"><span class=\"toc-no\">23</span>Above, on or below the SML</button></li><li data-full=\"24. Efficient frontier versus Security Market Line\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5n-s24\" title=\"24. Efficient frontier versus Security Market Line\"><span class=\"toc-no\">24</span>Efficient frontier vs SML</button></li><li data-full=\"25. Traditional portfolio management\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5n-s25\" title=\"25. Traditional portfolio management\"><span class=\"toc-no\">25</span>Traditional portfolio management</button></li><li data-full=\"26. Modern Portfolio Theory\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5n-s26\" title=\"26. Modern Portfolio Theory\"><span class=\"toc-no\">26</span>Modern Portfolio Theory</button></li><li data-full=\"27. The efficient frontier\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5n-s27\" title=\"27. The efficient frontier\"><span class=\"toc-no\">27</span>The efficient frontier</button></li><li data-full=\"28. Understanding Figure 5.7\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5n-s28\" title=\"28. Understanding Figure 5.7\"><span class=\"toc-no\">28</span>Understanding Figure 5.7</button></li><li data-full=\"29. Does everyone choose the same efficient portfolio?\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5n-s29\" title=\"29. Does everyone choose the same efficient portfolio?\"><span class=\"toc-no\">29</span>Different investors, different portfolios</button></li><li data-full=\"30. Portfolio beta\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5n-s30\" title=\"30. Portfolio beta\"><span class=\"toc-no\">30</span>Portfolio beta</button></li><li data-full=\"31. The Austin Fund portfolio-beta example\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5n-s31\" title=\"31. The Austin Fund portfolio-beta example\"><span class=\"toc-no\">31</span>Austin Fund portfolio betas</button></li><li data-full=\"32. Reconcile traditional portfolio management and MPT\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5n-s32\" title=\"32. Reconcile traditional portfolio management and MPT\"><span class=\"toc-no\">32</span>Reconciling traditional and MPT</button></li><li data-full=\"33. A practical portfolio-construction framework\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5n-s33\" title=\"33. A practical portfolio-construction framework\"><span class=\"toc-no\">33</span>A portfolio-construction framework</button></li><li data-full=\"34. Evaluate: &quot;Adding more stocks always reduces risk&quot;\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5n-s34\" title=\"34. Evaluate: &quot;Adding more stocks always reduces risk&quot;\"><span class=\"toc-no\">34</span>Evaluate: more stocks, less risk</button></li><li data-full=\"35. Evaluate: &quot;A stock with beta 0.5 is safer than a stock with beta 1.5&quot;\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5n-s35\" title=\"35. Evaluate: &quot;A stock with beta 0.5 is safer than a stock with beta 1.5&quot;\"><span class=\"toc-no\">35</span>Evaluate: low beta means safer</button></li><li data-full=\"36. Evaluate: &quot;An investor should buy the highest-beta stock to earn the highest return&quot;\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5n-s36\" title=\"36. Evaluate: &quot;An investor should buy the highest-beta stock to earn the highest return&quot;\"><span class=\"toc-no\">36</span>Evaluate: buy the highest beta</button></li><li data-full=\"37. Evaluate: &quot;A portfolio below the efficient frontier should be selected because it feels safer&quot;\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5n-s37\" title=\"37. Evaluate: &quot;A portfolio below the efficient frontier should be selected because it feels safer&quot;\"><span class=\"toc-no\">37</span>Evaluate: below the frontier feels safer</button></li><li data-full=\"38. Evaluate: &quot;Diversification eliminates risk&quot;\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5n-s38\" title=\"38. Evaluate: &quot;Diversification eliminates risk&quot;\"><span class=\"toc-no\">38</span>Evaluate: diversification eliminates risk</button></li><li data-full=\"39. A complete integrated investment case\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5n-s39\" title=\"39. A complete integrated investment case\"><span class=\"toc-no\">39</span>Integrated case</button></li><li data-full=\"40. Bloom&#x27;s Taxonomy: what you should actually be capable of doing\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5n-s40\" title=\"40. Bloom&#x27;s Taxonomy: what you should actually be capable of doing\"><span class=\"toc-no\">40</span>Bloom levels for Chapter 5</button></li><li data-full=\"41. Difficult exam-style questions\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5n-s41\" title=\"41. Difficult exam-style questions\"><span class=\"toc-no\">41</span>Difficult exam-style questions</button></li><li data-full=\"42. The formula sheet you should know\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5n-s42\" title=\"42. The formula sheet you should know\"><span class=\"toc-no\">42</span>The formula sheet you should know</button></li><li data-full=\"43. The five concepts I would prioritize hardest for the exam\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5n-s43\" title=\"43. The five concepts I would prioritize hardest for the exam\"><span class=\"toc-no\">43</span>Five concepts to master</button></li><li data-full=\"44. The deepest lesson of Chapter 5\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5n-s44\" title=\"44. The deepest lesson of Chapter 5\"><span class=\"toc-no\">44</span>The deepest lesson of Chapter 5</button></li></ul></details><details class=\"full-notes theme-l5\" open><summary><div><strong>Chapter 5 - complete intuitive explanation</strong><small>Every section of the notes, with worked numbers, maths and interactive visuals.</small></div><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"markdown-body\"><div class=\"notes-toolbar\"><button type=\"button\" data-sections=\"open\">Expand all</button><button type=\"button\" data-sections=\"close\">Collapse all</button><span>44 sections · click a heading to open it</span></div><h2>Chapter 5 - Modern Portfolio Concepts</h2>\n<p>Chapter 5 is arguably the biggest conceptual advancement in the investment course so far. It introduces a rather counterintuitive principle:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>You can sometimes reduce investment risk without sacrificing expected return, simply by changing how investments are combined.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>In Chapter 4, we evaluated investments individually using expected returns, standard deviations and risk premiums.</p>\n<p>Chapter 5 challenges that approach.</p>\n<p>A stock might be extremely risky when held alone, yet still improve a portfolio when combined with other assets.</p>\n<p>The central question is no longer:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Which investment has the best risk-return relationship?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>It becomes:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Which combination of investments produces the best risk-return relationship for the entire portfolio?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>This distinction is the foundation of Modern Portfolio Theory (MPT).</p>\n<p>The chapter covers portfolio returns and standard deviation, correlation and diversification, international diversification, systematic and unsystematic risk, beta, the Capital Asset Pricing Model (CAPM), the Security Market Line (SML), the efficient frontier, and traditional versus modern portfolio management.</p>\n<p>We will follow the chapter's concepts and examples, but also derive the mathematics, evaluate their assumptions, and apply them to investment decisions.</p>\n<p><strong>The entire chapter in one framework:</strong></p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">  1. Individual assets ........ expected return + risk + correlation\n          │\n          ▼\n  2. Portfolio construction ... weights + diversification\n          │\n          ▼\n  3. Efficient portfolios ..... maximum expected return for an acceptable risk\n          │\n          ▼\n  4. CAPM and portfolio beta .. is the expected return enough compensation\n                                for the SYSTEMATIC risk carried?\n</code></pre><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5n-s1\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">1. What is a portfolio, economically?</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Slide 4 defines a portfolio as a collection of investments assembled to satisfy one or more investment goals. It distinguishes growth-oriented portfolios, emphasizing long-term price appreciation, from income-oriented portfolios, emphasizing regular dividend and interest income.</p>\n<p>Suppose you have RM100,000.</p>\n<p>You could invest everything in one stock, or allocate your capital across several assets.</p>\n<p>Consider:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Asset</th>\n<th>Amount</th>\n<th>Weight</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Equity A</td>\n<td>RM50,000</td>\n<td>50%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Equity B</td>\n<td>RM30,000</td>\n<td>30%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bonds</td>\n<td>RM20,000</td>\n<td>20%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Total</td>\n<td>RM100,000</td>\n<td>100%</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>You now own a portfolio.</p>\n<p>But owning multiple investments does not automatically mean you have diversified effectively.</p>\n<p>Imagine you own five different Malaysian banks.</p>\n<p>You have five stocks, but all five may be vulnerable to the same economic events: weaker credit conditions, rising defaults, regulatory changes and changing interest rates.</p>\n<p>Compare that with holding stocks and bonds exposed to different economic forces.</p>\n<p>Both arrangements contain multiple securities, but their risk behavior can differ substantially.</p>\n<p>This introduces our first major lesson:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Number of assets}\\neq\\text{quality of diversification}}$$</div>\n<p>The relationships among the assets are more important than simply counting how many you own.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5n-s2\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">2. The ultimate goal: an efficient portfolio</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Slide 5 defines an efficient portfolio as one providing the highest expected return for a given level of risk.</p>\n<p>This gives investment management an optimization objective.</p>\n<p>Suppose you can construct three portfolios:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Portfolio</th>\n<th>Expected return</th>\n<th>Standard deviation</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A</td>\n<td>8%</td>\n<td>10%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B</td>\n<td>10%</td>\n<td>10%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>C</td>\n<td>12%</td>\n<td>20%</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Compare A and B.</p>\n<p>Both carry the same measured risk:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\sigma_A=\\sigma_B=10\\%$$</div>\n<p>But B gives:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$E(R_B)=10\\%&gt;8\\%$$</div>\n<p>There is no reason to choose A over B if the return and risk measurements capture all relevant differences.</p>\n<p>B dominates A.</p>\n<p>Now compare B and C.</p>\n<p>C gives more expected return, but also more risk.</p>\n<p>Neither necessarily dominates the other.</p>\n<p>A risk-averse investor might prefer B, whereas an investor with greater risk tolerance might prefer C.</p>\n<p>The critical distinction is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{An efficient portfolio need not have the highest absolute return}}$$</div>\n<p>Instead, it offers an efficient tradeoff between return and risk.</p>\n<p>Modern Portfolio Theory attempts to identify these efficient combinations mathematically.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5n-s3\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">3. Portfolio return: surprisingly simple</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The slide on slide 6 makes a distinction that you should remember throughout the chapter:</p>\n<p>Portfolio expected return is relatively easy to calculate. Portfolio risk is more complicated.</p>\n<p>For expected return:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{E(R_p)=\\sum_{i=1}^{n}w_iE(R_i)}$$</div>\n<p>where:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>$E(R_p)$ = expected portfolio return</li>\n<li>$w_i$ = proportion invested in asset $i$</li>\n<li>$E(R_i)$ = expected return of asset $i$</li>\n</ul>\n<p>And:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\sum_{i=1}^{n}w_i=1$$</div>\n<p>for a fully invested portfolio.</p>\n<h4>Worked example</h4>\n<p>Suppose:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Asset</th>\n<th>Weight</th>\n<th>Expected return</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A</td>\n<td>50%</td>\n<td>12%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B</td>\n<td>30%</td>\n<td>8%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>C</td>\n<td>20%</td>\n<td>5%</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Then:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$E(R_p)=0.50(12\\%)+0.30(8\\%)+0.20(5\\%)$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$=6\\%+2.4\\%+1\\%$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{E(R_p)=9.4\\%}$$</div>\n<p>That's a weighted average.</p>\n<p>The economic interpretation is straightforward: each asset contributes to the portfolio's expected return in proportion to how much capital you have allocated to it.</p>\n<h4>Why this matters</h4>\n<p>If you allocate 90% of your wealth to a particular stock, its return dominates your portfolio even if you own 20 other investments.</p>\n<p>Portfolio weights reveal your actual economic exposure.</p>\n<p>So when evaluating a portfolio, never look only at its list of holdings. Look at the capital allocated to each.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5n-s4\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">4. Portfolio risk: why we cannot just average standard deviations</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Here's where Chapter 5 becomes genuinely interesting.</p>\n<p>Suppose you have two assets:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\sigma_A=20\\%$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\sigma_B=10\\%$$</div>\n<p>If you allocate 50% to each, can you simply calculate:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\sigma_p=0.5(20\\%)+0.5(10\\%)=15\\%$$</div>\n<p>Not generally.</p>\n<p>That calculation assumes their returns are perfectly positively correlated.</p>\n<p>But what if Asset A tends to perform well exactly when Asset B performs poorly?</p>\n<p>Then the losses or fluctuations of one asset can be partially offset by the other.</p>\n<p>The chapter explains that portfolio standard deviation depends on asset weights, individual standard deviations and correlations between the assets.</p>\n<p>This produces the most important insight of the entire chapter:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{ \\text{Portfolio risk depends not only on how risky assets are, but also on how they move together.} }$$</div>\n<p>That is the reason diversification works.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5n-s5\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">5. Correlation: why diversification actually works</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The chapter defines correlation as the statistical relationship between two sets of returns. Positively correlated assets tend to move in the same direction, while negatively correlated assets tend to move in opposite directions.</p>\n<p>The correlation coefficient, represented by $\\rho$, ranges from:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{-1\\le\\rho\\le+1}$$</div>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Correlation</th>\n<th>Interpretation</th>\n<th>Diversification potential</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>+1.0</td>\n<td>Perfectly positive</td>\n<td>No reduction from imperfect co-movement</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>+0.5</td>\n<td>Moderately positive</td>\n<td>Some</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>0</td>\n<td>No linear relationship</td>\n<td>Potentially substantial</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>-0.5</td>\n<td>Moderately negative</td>\n<td>Greater</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>-1.0</td>\n<td>Perfectly negative</td>\n<td>Can eliminate variance with appropriate weights</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Think of correlation as asking:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>When Asset A moves away from its average, what does Asset B tend to do?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>That is different from asking whether the assets individually generate good returns.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5n-s6\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">6. Why negative correlation can reduce portfolio risk</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"l5_offset\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>When one asset is up the other is down: the combined return barely moves.</figcaption></figure></div><p>Suppose two hypothetical assets behave like this:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Economic condition</th>\n<th>Asset A</th>\n<th>Asset B</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Strong economy</td>\n<td>+20%</td>\n<td>0%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Weak economy</td>\n<td>0%</td>\n<td>+20%</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>If you invest 50% in each:</p>\n<p>During a strong economy:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R_p=0.5(20\\%)+0.5(0\\%)=10\\%$$</div>\n<p>During a weak economy:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R_p=0.5(0\\%)+0.5(20\\%)=10\\%$$</div>\n<p>Your portfolio generates:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{10\\%\\text{ in both scenarios}}$$</div>\n<p>Although each individual asset has uncertain returns, the portfolio return is constant across these two scenarios.</p>\n<p>This is diversification at work.</p>\n<p>You haven't eliminated the fluctuations inside the individual assets.</p>\n<p>You've combined them so that their fluctuations offset each other.</p>\n<p>The diagram on slide 12 illustrates this exact principle: Asset F and Asset G fluctuate noticeably, but the combined portfolio has a much smoother return pattern.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5n-s7\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">7. The mathematical formula for portfolio risk</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"l5_sigma3d\"><div class=\"vis-title\">Portfolio risk surface in 3D</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>3D · Portfolio standard deviation for every weight and correlation: the valley only reaches zero at ρ = -1.</figcaption></figure></div><p>This is the most technical calculation in the chapter.</p>\n<p>For two assets:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{ \\sigma_p= \\sqrt{ w_A^2\\sigma_A^2+ w_B^2\\sigma_B^2+ 2w_Aw_B\\sigma_A\\sigma_B\\rho_{AB} } }$$</div>\n<p>Where:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>$\\sigma_p$: portfolio standard deviation</li>\n<li>$w_A,w_B$: asset weights</li>\n<li>$\\sigma_A,\\sigma_B$: individual asset standard deviations</li>\n<li>$\\rho_{AB}$: correlation between their returns</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This formula is also used in the worked example in Table 5.2 on slide 45.</p>\n<h4>Understanding each component</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Term</th>\n<th>Name</th>\n<th>What it captures</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>$w_A^2\\sigma_A^2+w_B^2\\sigma_B^2$</td>\n<td>Individual risk contributions</td>\n<td>How much variability each asset contributes, accounting for its portfolio weight</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>$2w_Aw_B\\sigma_A\\sigma_B\\rho_{AB}$</td>\n<td>Co-movement contribution</td>\n<td>How the assets' movements reinforce or offset one another</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The second term explains why diversification works.</p>\n<p>If:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\rho_{AB}&gt;0$$</div>\n<p>the term increases portfolio variance.</p>\n<p>If:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\rho_{AB}&lt;0$$</div>\n<p>the term reduces portfolio variance.</p>\n<p>That is the mathematical mechanism.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5n-s8\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">8. Worked example: same assets, different correlations</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"l5_correlation\"><div class=\"vis-title\">Correlation and portfolio risk</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · Slide the weight and the correlation; the risk-return curve bends left as correlation falls.</figcaption></figure></div><p>Suppose:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$w_A=w_B=0.5$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\sigma_A=20\\%,\\qquad \\sigma_B=10\\%$$</div>\n<p>Their individual volatility is fixed. Only their correlation changes.</p>\n<p><strong>Portfolio volatility as correlation changes</strong> (equal-weighted portfolio, asset volatilities of 20% and 10%):</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\"> correlation ρ    portfolio σ\n     +1.0   ███████████████   15.00%\n     +0.5   █████████████     13.23%\n      0.0   ███████████       11.18%\n     -0.5   █████████          8.66%\n     -1.0   █████              5.00%\n            0%   5%   10%  15%\n</code></pre>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Correlation</th>\n<th>Portfolio σ</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>+1.0</td>\n<td>15.00%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>+0.5</td>\n<td>13.23%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>0</td>\n<td>11.18%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>-0.5</td>\n<td>8.66%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>-1.0</td>\n<td>5.00%</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>With perfectly positive correlation:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\sigma_p=15\\%$$</div>\n<p>With zero correlation:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\sigma_p\\approx11.18\\%$$</div>\n<p>With perfectly negative correlation:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\sigma_p=5\\%$$</div>\n<p>Neither asset became individually safer.</p>\n<p>The portfolio became safer because of how their returns interacted.</p>\n<p>This is what Figure 5.4 on slide 14 illustrates graphically: reducing correlation can move the available portfolio combinations toward lower risk for comparable expected returns.</p>\n<h4>A subtle but important qualification</h4>\n<p>The slide on slide 11 says perfectly negative correlation can eliminate all risk. That needs a condition: the portfolio weights must also be appropriate.</p>\n<p>With standard deviations of 20% and 10%, a 50/50 portfolio still has 5% volatility at correlation -1.</p>\n<p>To eliminate volatility in this idealized example, weights must satisfy:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$w_A\\sigma_A=w_B\\sigma_B$$</div>\n<p>Since the weights sum to one, this gives:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{w_A=\\frac13,\\qquad w_B=\\frac23}$$</div>\n<p>This is the difference between understanding negative correlation and actually constructing the right hedge.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5n-s9\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">9. The chapter's X, Y and Z example: an especially important table</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Table 5.3 on slide 46 provides a powerful illustration of diversification.</p>\n<p>It contains three hypothetical assets with the following expected returns:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Year</th>\n<th>X</th>\n<th>Y</th>\n<th>Z</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>2015</td>\n<td>8%</td>\n<td>24%</td>\n<td>8%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2016</td>\n<td>10%</td>\n<td>20%</td>\n<td>12%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2017</td>\n<td>12%</td>\n<td>16%</td>\n<td>16%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2018</td>\n<td>14%</td>\n<td>12%</td>\n<td>20%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2019</td>\n<td>16%</td>\n<td>8%</td>\n<td>24%</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Source: Chapter 5, Table 5.3.</p>\n<p>Notice that X and Y move in opposite directions.</p>\n<p>As X increases from 8% to 16%, Y declines from 24% to 8%.</p>\n<p>Now construct a portfolio with:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$w_X=\\frac23$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$w_Y=\\frac13$$</div>\n<p>In 2015:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R_{XY}=\\frac23(8\\%)+\\frac13(24\\%)$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$=13.33\\%$$</div>\n<p>In 2016:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R_{XY}=\\frac23(10\\%)+\\frac13(20\\%)$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$=13.33\\%$$</div>\n<p>This holds for every year in the table.</p>\n<p>Therefore:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{E(R_{XY})\\approx13.33\\%}$$</div>\n<p>and:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\sigma_{XY}=0\\%}$$</div>\n<p>Now compare with combining X and Z using the same weights.</p>\n<p>The chapter reports:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$E(R_{XZ})\\approx13.33\\%$$</div>\n<p>but:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\sigma_{XZ}=4.2\\%$$</div>\n<p><strong>The two portfolio outcomes:</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Portfolio</th>\n<th>Expected return</th>\n<th>Standard deviation</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>XY ($\\tfrac23$ X, $\\tfrac13$ Y)</td>\n<td>13.33%</td>\n<td><strong>0%</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>XZ ($\\tfrac23$ X, $\\tfrac13$ Z)</td>\n<td>13.33%</td>\n<td><strong>4.2%</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Which portfolio is superior under the chapter's risk measure?</p>\n<p>XY.</p>\n<p>Both have the same expected return, but XY has lower risk.</p>\n<p>This is a clean demonstration of portfolio dominance.</p>\n<p>Of course, the zero-risk outcome relies on the hypothetical return relationships remaining exact. Real-world correlations are estimated and can change.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5n-s10\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">10. Why portfolio risk is not the weighted average of asset risks</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Chapter 4 taught us to measure an individual asset's risk using standard deviation.</p>\n<p>Chapter 5 teaches us that a portfolio's standard deviation depends on:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{weights}+\\text{volatilities}+\\text{correlations}}$$</div>\n<p>Consider the chapter's IBM and Celgene example.</p>\n<p>Table 5.1 reports approximately:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Investment</th>\n<th>Average return</th>\n<th>Standard deviation</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>IBM</td>\n<td>10.4%</td>\n<td>28.6%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Celgene</td>\n<td>32.1%</td>\n<td>58.3%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Portfolio: 86% IBM, 14% Celgene</td>\n<td>13.5%</td>\n<td>25.3%</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Notice what happened:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\sigma_{\\text{portfolio}}=25.3\\%$$</div>\n<p>This is lower than IBM's individual standard deviation:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$28.6\\%$$</div>\n<p>despite adding Celgene, which has a much higher standard deviation of 58.3%.</p>\n<p>This is an important conceptual breakthrough.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Adding a riskier asset can reduce total portfolio risk.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>That is possible when its covariance with the existing assets provides enough diversification benefit.</p>\n<p>The correct question is not simply:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Is Celgene risky?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>It is:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>How does adding Celgene change the risk of the portfolio I already own?</p>\n</blockquote></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5n-s11\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">11. International diversification: a different source of correlation benefits</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The slides explain that international investments can create additional diversification opportunities because economic cycles in different countries may not move together. They also recognize currency risk, costs and investment convenience as disadvantages.</p>\n<p>Imagine a Malaysian investor who holds only Malaysian equities.</p>\n<p>If Malaysia experiences a prolonged domestic recession, many of those investments may be adversely affected.</p>\n<p>Now suppose that investor also holds exposures to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>U.S. equities;</li>\n<li>Japanese equities;</li>\n<li>European bonds.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The economic conditions affecting these securities may differ.</p>\n<p>This can reduce overall portfolio concentration.</p>\n<p>But the benefit is not automatic.</p>\n<h4>Why international diversification can fail</h4>\n<p>Suppose Malaysia and the United States experience a global financial crisis simultaneously.</p>\n<p>Equity markets may become highly correlated during the crisis.</p>\n<p>Therefore:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Historical low correlation}\\neq\\text{guaranteed future low correlation}}$$</div>\n<p>Foreign assets can also introduce currency risk.</p>\n<p>For Malaysian investors, a foreign asset's MYR return depends on both the asset's return in its currency and exchange-rate movements.</p>\n<p>Thus, international diversification must be evaluated after considering currency effects and other costs, not merely by counting countries.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5n-s12\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">12. Systematic versus unsystematic risk</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>We now reach the second major foundation of the chapter.</p>\n<p>The slides divide total risk into two components.</p>\n<p>Unsystematic risk arises from firm-specific factors such as lawsuits or labor strikes and can be reduced through diversification.</p>\n<p>Systematic risk arises from broader forces such as inflation, war and political events that affect many investments simultaneously.</p>\n<p>Conceptually:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{ \\text{Total Risk} = \\text{Systematic Risk} + \\text{Unsystematic Risk} }$$</div>\n<p>This is the decomposition shown on slide 19.</p>\n<h4>Unsystematic risk: company-specific</h4>\n<p>Imagine that Company A loses its CEO unexpectedly.</p>\n<p>Its share price falls sharply.</p>\n<p>But Company B, C and D might be unaffected.</p>\n<p>If your entire wealth is invested in A, the shock could be disastrous.</p>\n<p>If A represents only 2% of a diversified portfolio, the portfolio impact may be comparatively small.</p>\n<p>The event risk associated with A has been diluted.</p>\n<h4>Systematic risk: market-wide</h4>\n<p>Now suppose interest rates rise sharply and the broader economy enters recession.</p>\n<p>Many companies experience lower demand and changes in valuation simultaneously.</p>\n<p>Owning more stocks does not necessarily protect you from that common market shock.</p>\n<p>This is systematic risk.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5n-s13\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">13. Why diversification cannot remove all market risk</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"l5_risk_floor\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>Adding securities quickly removes firm-specific risk, then levels off at the systematic-risk floor.</figcaption></figure></div><p>The diagram on slide 35 is especially important.</p>\n<p><strong>Figure 5.8 - Portfolio Risk and Diversification.</strong> The graph shows portfolio risk declining as the number of securities increases, before gradually approaching a lower boundary.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\"> portfolio\n risk (σ)\n   ▲\n   │●\n   │ ●                          ← diversifiable (unsystematic) risk\n   │  ●                            shrinks as securities are added\n   │    ●\n   │       ●\n   │           ●  ●\n   │                  ●   ●    ●     ●      ●       ●\n   │ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─\n   │                                ← nondiversifiable (systematic) risk\n   │                                   remains: the risk floor\n   └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────► number of securities\n</code></pre>\n<p><em>Conceptual recreation of the chapter's diversification curve, not empirical data.</em></p>\n<p>Early diversification can produce substantial risk reduction.</p>\n<p>But as you add more securities, the incremental benefit generally diminishes.</p>\n<p>Eventually you approach a risk floor associated with common market exposures.</p>\n<p>This is why:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{ \\text{Diversification removes firm-specific risk, not all systematic risk.} }$$</div>\n<p>The real challenge is therefore deciding how much systematic risk you want to accept.</p>\n<p>That leads us to beta.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5n-s14\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">14. Beta: measuring sensitivity to market movements</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Chapter 4 used standard deviation as a general risk measure.</p>\n<p>Chapter 5 introduces beta, which focuses on systematic market risk.</p>\n<p>The chapter defines beta as the measure of how a security responds to market forces and uses a broad stock index such as the S&amp;P 500 as the market benchmark. The market itself has a beta of 1.</p>\n<p>Beta is commonly represented as:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\beta}$$</div>\n<p>Interpretation:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Beta</th>\n<th>Market sensitivity</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>2.0</td>\n<td>Approximately twice the market's movement component</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1.0</td>\n<td>Same sensitivity as the market</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>0.5</td>\n<td>Half the market sensitivity</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>0</td>\n<td>No linear sensitivity to the benchmark</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>-1.0</td>\n<td>Opposite market sensitivity</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The slide on slide 21 presents these examples, including negative betas.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5n-s15\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">15. Beta is not the same thing as standard deviation</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>This distinction is absolutely essential.</p>\n<p>Standard deviation asks:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>How much does this asset's return fluctuate?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Beta asks:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>How sensitive is this asset's return to movements in the overall market?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>For example, consider an asset that experiences large fluctuations caused primarily by company-specific developments.</p>\n<p>It might have:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\sigma=40\\%$$</div>\n<p>but:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\beta=0.5$$</div>\n<p>Its total volatility is high, but its sensitivity to the market factor is relatively low.</p>\n<p>Another asset might have:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\sigma=25\\%$$</div>\n<p>and:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\beta=1.3$$</div>\n<p>It has lower total volatility but greater exposure to market movements.</p>\n<p>Therefore:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Standard deviation measures total volatility}}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Beta measures market sensitivity}}$$</div>\n<p>Neither metric replaces the other for every purpose.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5n-s16\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">16. Where beta comes from mathematically</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>This equation is an additional mathematical derivation of the beta concept introduced in the slides:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{ \\beta_i= \\frac{\\operatorname{Cov}(R_i,R_m)} {\\operatorname{Var}(R_m)} }$$</div>\n<p>where:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>$R_i$ = asset return</li>\n<li>$R_m$ = market return</li>\n<li>Cov = covariance</li>\n<li>Var = variance</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The intuition is that beta depends on how strongly an asset's returns move with the market, relative to the market's own variance.</p>\n<p>An equivalent form is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{ \\beta_i= \\rho_{i,m}\\frac{\\sigma_i}{\\sigma_m} }$$</div>\n<p>This equation connects three things you have learned:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Correlation} + \\text{Individual volatility} + \\text{Market volatility}$$</div>\n<p>to produce beta.</p>\n<h4>Example</h4>\n<p>Suppose:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\rho_{i,m}=0.75$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\sigma_i=30\\%$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\sigma_m=15\\%$$</div>\n<p>Then:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\beta_i=0.75\\left(\\frac{30}{15}\\right)$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\beta_i=1.5}$$</div>\n<p>So the stock has 1.5 times the market sensitivity.</p>\n<p>This is why you cannot determine beta using the stock's standard deviation alone.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5n-s17\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">17. Interpreting beta correctly</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The chapter illustrates that a stock with:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\beta=1.5$$</div>\n<p>has a market-related response approximately 1.5 times that of the benchmark.</p>\n<p>Suppose the market's return changes by:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$+10\\%$$</div>\n<p>Its estimated market-related response is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$1.5(10\\%)=15\\%$$</div>\n<p>Similarly, with:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\beta=0.5$$</div>\n<p>a market movement of:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$-8\\%$$</div>\n<p>corresponds to approximately:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$0.5(-8\\%)=-4\\%$$</div>\n<p>However, beta is not a guarantee of the security's actual return.</p>\n<p>A stock can have beta 1.5 and still decline when the market rises because company-specific events can overwhelm the market component.</p>\n<p>More precisely, beta describes a linear relationship estimated from returns. It is not a mechanical multiplier that guarantees every realized price movement.</p>\n<p>This distinction matters when evaluating the reliability of the model.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5n-s18\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">18. Why only systematic risk is rewarded under CAPM</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Now we reach one of the deepest theoretical ideas in the chapter.</p>\n<p>The slides explain that only nondiversifiable risk is treated as relevant for the positive risk-return relationship in portfolio pricing.</p>\n<p>Why?</p>\n<p>Imagine two companies.</p>\n<p>Company A has high risk because its operations are unusually unpredictable.</p>\n<p>Company B has lower company-specific risk.</p>\n<p>If investors can cheaply diversify away much of A's firm-specific risk, why should the market consistently compensate them for voluntarily holding that risk in a concentrated portfolio?</p>\n<p>Under the CAPM framework, the market compensates investors for bearing systematic risk, which cannot be eliminated simply by holding a broad portfolio of risky securities.</p>\n<p>Therefore:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{More total risk does not automatically justify a higher CAPM return}}$$</div>\n<p>What matters to CAPM is the asset's contribution to market-related risk.</p>\n<p>This is an assumption-driven theoretical result, not a promise that every market asset will behave accordingly.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5n-s19\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">19. The Capital Asset Pricing Model (CAPM)</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The slides define CAPM as a model linking risk and return to determine an investment's required return. It uses beta, the risk-free rate and the expected market return.</p>\n<p>The formula is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{ R_i=R_f+\\beta_i(E(R_m)-R_f) }$$</div>\n<p>Where:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>$R_i$: CAPM-required return on the investment</li>\n<li>$R_f$: risk-free rate</li>\n<li>$\\beta_i$: security's beta</li>\n<li>$E(R_m)$: expected market return</li>\n</ul>\n<p>You should recognize the expression:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$E(R_m)-R_f$$</div>\n<p>as the market risk premium.</p>\n<p>Thus:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{ \\text{Required Return} = \\text{Risk-Free Rate} + \\text{Beta-Adjusted Market Risk Premium} }$$</div></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5n-s20\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">20. Deriving the economic logic of CAPM</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Suppose:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R_f=4\\%$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$E(R_m)=10\\%$$</div>\n<p>The market risk premium is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$10\\%-4\\%=6\\%$$</div>\n<p>The market offers 6 percentage points of expected compensation beyond the risk-free rate.</p>\n<p>Now consider three investments.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Investment</th>\n<th>Beta</th>\n<th>Required return</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A</td>\n<td>0.5</td>\n<td>7%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B</td>\n<td>1.0</td>\n<td>10%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>C</td>\n<td>1.5</td>\n<td>13%</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>For C:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R_C=4\\%+1.5(10\\%-4\\%)$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$=4\\%+9\\%$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{R_C=13\\%}$$</div>\n<p>Why 13%?</p>\n<p>Because this investment has 1.5 times the market sensitivity, so CAPM assigns it 1.5 times the market risk premium.</p>\n<p>The risk-free rate is the baseline return; beta determines the additional required compensation.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5n-s21\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">21. What happens if beta is zero or negative?</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Suppose:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R_f=4\\%,\\qquad E(R_m)=10\\%$$</div>\n<p>If:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\beta=0$$</div>\n<p>then:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R=4\\%+0(6\\%)=4\\%$$</div>\n<p>If:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\beta=-0.5$$</div>\n<p>then:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R=4\\%-0.5(6\\%)$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{R=1\\%}$$</div>\n<p>Why would CAPM assign a lower required return to an asset with negative beta?</p>\n<p>Because negative-beta exposure can potentially provide protection against market movements.</p>\n<p>It may have a hedging benefit.</p>\n<p>Investors may therefore accept a lower expected return in exchange for that protection.</p>\n<p>Notice how this connects with the earlier diversification logic.</p>\n<p>An asset need not deliver a high individual expected return to be economically valuable inside a portfolio.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5n-s22\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">22. The Security Market Line (SML)</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"l5_sml\"><div class=\"vis-title\">CAPM and the Security Market Line</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · Set the risk-free rate, market return, beta and your expected return; compare it with the SML.</figcaption></figure></div><p>The chapter describes the SML as the graphical representation of CAPM.</p>\n<p>It plots:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Required Return}$$</div>\n<p>against:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Beta}$$</div>\n<p>Using the chapter's Figure 5.6 example, the risk-free rate is 6%, and the required return at beta 1 is 10%.</p>\n<p><strong>Security Market Line</strong> (illustrative CAPM using a 6% risk-free rate and a 10% market return):</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\"> required\n return\n  16% ┤                               ● β = 2.5 → 16%\n      │                            ·\n  14% ┤                         ● β = 2.0 → 14%\n      │                      ·\n  12% ┤                   ● β = 1.5 → 12%\n      │                ·\n  10% ┤             ● β = 1.0 → 10% (the market)\n      │          ·\n   8% ┤       ● β = 0.5 → 8%\n      │    ·\n   6% ┤ ● Rf (β = 0)\n      └──────────────────────────────────► beta (β)\n             0.5    1    1.5    2    2.5\n\n  R_i = 6% + 4%·β  →  every extra 0.5 of beta adds 2% of required return\n</code></pre>\n<p>The equation is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R_i=6\\%+\\beta_i(10\\%-6\\%)$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{R_i=6\\%+4\\%\\beta_i}$$</div>\n<p>The graph therefore has:</p>\n<p>Intercept:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R_f=6\\%$$</div>\n<p>Slope:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$E(R_m)-R_f=4\\%$$</div>\n<p>The intercept gives the return required at zero market beta.</p>\n<p>The slope indicates how much additional required return accompanies each additional unit of beta.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5n-s23\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">23. Above, on or below the Security Market Line</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"l5_sml_sketch\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>Securities above the SML offer more than CAPM requires for their beta; below it, less.</figcaption></figure></div><p>This is where CAPM becomes a valuation decision tool.</p>\n<p>Suppose a security has:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\beta=1.5$$</div>\n<p>With:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R_f=4\\%,\\qquad E(R_m)=10\\%$$</div>\n<p>CAPM-required return is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R_{\\text{CAPM}}=13\\%$$</div>\n<p>Now suppose your own analysis estimates that its expected return is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$15\\%$$</div>\n<p>Its expected return exceeds the compensation required by CAPM.</p>\n<p>The difference is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\alpha=15\\%-13\\%$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\alpha=+2\\%}$$</div>\n<p>This is positive expected alpha relative to CAPM.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Position</th>\n<th>Interpretation</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Above SML</td>\n<td>Expected return exceeds CAPM-required return; potentially attractive</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>On SML</td>\n<td>Expected return matches CAPM-required return</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Below SML</td>\n<td>Expected return is insufficient relative to CAPM-required return</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>A security above the SML may be interpreted as undervalued relative to the model and forecasts.</p>\n<p>A security below it may be interpreted as overvalued relative to those assumptions.</p>\n<p>But be careful:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Positive alpha is not proof that you discovered an undervalued investment.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>It could simply reflect inaccurate expected-return estimates or an inadequate pricing model.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5n-s24\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">24. Efficient frontier versus Security Market Line</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>These two concepts are often confused.</p>\n<p>They answer different questions.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Efficient frontier</th>\n<th>SML</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Horizontal axis</td>\n<td>Portfolio standard deviation</td>\n<td>Beta</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Vertical axis</td>\n<td>Expected return</td>\n<td>Required/expected equilibrium return</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Central question</td>\n<td>Which portfolio combinations are efficient?</td>\n<td>Is return adequate for systematic risk?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Principal theory</td>\n<td>Portfolio optimization</td>\n<td>CAPM</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>So:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Efficient Frontier = Portfolio Construction}}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{SML = Risk-Based Pricing Benchmark}}$$</div>\n<p>One is about combining assets.</p>\n<p>The other is about the return required for a particular level of systematic risk.</p>\n<p>Understanding this distinction is much more important than just drawing two graphs from memory.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5n-s25\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">25. Traditional portfolio management</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The chapter distinguishes traditional portfolio management from MPT.</p>\n<p>The traditional approach emphasizes holding a variety of stocks and bonds across industries. It also tends to favor familiar, well-established companies because of perceived safety, liquidity and investor comfort.</p>\n<p>The basic philosophy is:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Don't put all your eggs in one basket.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Suppose you construct a portfolio of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>several banks;</li>\n<li>several technology companies;</li>\n<li>several manufacturers;</li>\n<li>several bonds.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>You are trying to spread exposure across sectors and securities.</p>\n<p>There is nothing inherently wrong with this.</p>\n<p>But how do you know whether the assets actually diversify each other?</p>\n<p>That's the limitation.</p>\n<p>Traditional diversification often relies more heavily on categories and judgment.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5n-s26\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">26. Modern Portfolio Theory</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>MPT takes a more statistical approach.</p>\n<p>The chapter identifies three core inputs:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Expected returns}}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Standard deviations}}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Correlations}}$$</div>\n<p>It emphasizes combining securities with negative or relatively low positive correlations.</p>\n<p>The important change is that diversification becomes something we can measure and optimize.</p>\n<p>Instead of saying:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>I own many different companies, so I must be diversified.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>MPT asks:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>What is the estimated covariance structure of these assets, and what combination achieves the best available tradeoff?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>That's a substantial improvement in analytical precision.</p>\n<p>However, it also introduces dependence on statistical estimates.</p>\n<p>If correlations and future expected returns are estimated poorly, the mathematically optimal portfolio can perform poorly in reality.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5n-s27\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">27. The efficient frontier</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"l5_frontier\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>Only the upper-left edge of the feasible set is efficient; the utility curve picks the investor&#x27;s point on it.</figcaption></figure></div><p>The chapter defines the efficient frontier as the boundary containing portfolios with the best attainable risk-return tradeoffs. Portfolios to the right of the frontier are inferior, while combinations outside the attainable set cannot be achieved under the assumed investment opportunity set.</p>\n<p>Imagine thousands of possible portfolios.</p>\n<p>Each has:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$E(R_p)$$</div>\n<p>and:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\sigma_p$$</div>\n<p>You plot all of them.</p>\n<p>Some portfolios have high risk but low expected return.</p>\n<p>Others offer better return at the same level of risk.</p>\n<p>The efficient frontier identifies the superior tradeoffs.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5n-s28\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">28. Understanding Figure 5.7</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The diagram on slide 32 shows a shaded feasible portfolio set and a highlighted efficient frontier. It also includes utility curves representing investor preferences.</p>\n<p>The axes are:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Vertical: Expected Return}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Horizontal: Portfolio Risk}$$</div>\n<p>A portfolio inside the feasible region may be achievable but inefficient.</p>\n<p>An investor could potentially find another portfolio delivering:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>the same return with less risk; or</li>\n<li>more return with the same risk.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Only portfolios on the efficient portion of the boundary avoid that kind of dominance.</p>\n<p>A technical refinement: in the usual mean-variance model, the upper portion of the minimum-variance boundary is the efficient frontier. The lowest-risk boundary by itself can also contain portfolios that are inefficient because another combination offers greater expected return for the same volatility.</p>\n<p>This is why portfolio optimization is not simply:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Minimize standard deviation.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>It is:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Minimize risk for the desired return, or maximize expected return for acceptable risk.</p>\n</blockquote></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5n-s29\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">29. Does everyone choose the same efficient portfolio?</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>No.</p>\n<p>Different investors have different risk preferences.</p>\n<p>Consider two portfolios on the frontier:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Portfolio A</th>\n<th>Portfolio B</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Expected return</td>\n<td>8%</td>\n<td>13%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Standard deviation</td>\n<td>7%</td>\n<td>18%</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Both might be efficient.</p>\n<p>An investor approaching retirement may prefer A.</p>\n<p>A younger investor with greater capacity to withstand investment volatility may prefer B.</p>\n<p>Neither is necessarily irrational.</p>\n<p>The frontier identifies efficient alternatives.</p>\n<p>Investor preferences determine which efficient alternative is appropriate.</p>\n<p>That is why Figure 5.7 includes utility curves: the desired portfolio depends on the investor's willingness to trade additional risk for additional return.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5n-s30\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">30. Portfolio beta</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The chapter defines portfolio beta as the weighted average of the individual assets' betas.</p>\n<p>The formula is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{ \\beta_p=\\sum_{i=1}^{n}w_i\\beta_i }$$</div>\n<p>Notice something interesting:</p>\n<p>Portfolio return is a weighted average.</p>\n<p>Portfolio beta is also a weighted average.</p>\n<p>But portfolio standard deviation is not generally a weighted average.</p>\n<p>That distinction is highly testable.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5n-s31\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">31. The Austin Fund portfolio-beta example</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The chapter provides two sample portfolios, V and W, on slide 35.</p>\n<p>Using Table 5.6:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Asset</th>\n<th>V Weight</th>\n<th>V Beta</th>\n<th>W Weight</th>\n<th>W Beta</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>0.10</td>\n<td>1.65</td>\n<td>0.10</td>\n<td>0.80</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>0.30</td>\n<td>1.00</td>\n<td>0.10</td>\n<td>1.00</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>0.20</td>\n<td>1.30</td>\n<td>0.20</td>\n<td>0.65</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>0.20</td>\n<td>1.10</td>\n<td>0.10</td>\n<td>0.75</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>0.20</td>\n<td>1.25</td>\n<td>0.50</td>\n<td>1.05</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>For Portfolio V:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\begin{aligned} \\beta_V={}&amp;0.10(1.65)+0.30(1.00)\\\\ &amp;+0.20(1.30)+0.20(1.10)\\\\ &amp;+0.20(1.25) \\end{aligned}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\beta_V=1.195}$$</div>\n<p>For Portfolio W:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\begin{aligned} \\beta_W={}&amp;0.10(0.80)+0.10(1.00)\\\\ &amp;+0.20(0.65)+0.10(0.75)\\\\ &amp;+0.50(1.05) \\end{aligned}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\beta_W=0.910}$$</div>\n<h4>Interpretation</h4>\n<p>Portfolio V has greater sensitivity to market movements than Portfolio W.</p>\n<p>If the market's return changes by 10 percentage points, the estimated market-linked changes are approximately:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$V:1.195(10\\%)=11.95\\text{ percentage points}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$W:0.910(10\\%)=9.10\\text{ percentage points}$$</div>\n<p>These are market-factor sensitivities, not guaranteed realized portfolio returns.</p>\n<p>This tells us W is less exposed to market movements, but we cannot infer their exact total standard deviations or expected returns from beta alone.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5n-s32\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">32. Reconcile traditional portfolio management and MPT</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The chapter recommends combining elements of both approaches. Its final management policy emphasizes risk tolerance, diversification across securities and industries, return correlations, portfolio beta and evaluating alternatives for the best acceptable return-risk tradeoff.</p>\n<p>This is a sensible conclusion.</p>\n<p>Traditional management provides practical economic judgment.</p>\n<p>MPT provides quantitative discipline.</p>\n<p>Neither is sufficient by itself.</p>\n<p>For example, an optimizer may identify an asset combination with excellent historical correlation properties.</p>\n<p>But suppose one asset is illiquid and extremely expensive to trade.</p>\n<p>A mathematically attractive portfolio might still be practically unsuitable.</p>\n<p>Likewise, a portfolio constructed only from familiar companies might miss substantial diversification opportunities.</p>\n<p>A strong investment process incorporates both.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5n-s33\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">33. A practical portfolio-construction framework</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>This is a practical extension of the chapter's concluding framework.</p>\n<p>Suppose you are managing RM100,000 for an investor.</p>\n<p>A defensible process would be:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Define the objective. Determine whether the investor wants long-term growth, income, capital preservation or a combination.</li>\n<li>Determine risk capacity and tolerance. Establish time horizon, liquidity needs and how much loss the investor can withstand.</li>\n<li>Estimate expected asset returns. Use valuation, economic analysis and historical evidence, recognizing that forecasts are uncertain.</li>\n<li>Measure risk and co-movement. Estimate each asset's standard deviation, correlations and market beta.</li>\n<li>Construct and compare portfolios. Identify combinations offering stronger expected return-risk tradeoffs rather than choosing assets independently.</li>\n<li>Test practicality and robustness. Consider trading costs, liquidity, currency exposure and how estimated correlations might change in unfavorable conditions.</li>\n<li>Choose and monitor. Select an efficient or near-efficient portfolio suitable for the investor and reassess exposures as circumstances change.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The goal is not to find the mathematically highest-return portfolio at any cost.</p>\n<p>It is to find a robust, financially suitable combination of investments.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5n-s34\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">34. Evaluate: \"Adding more stocks always reduces risk\"</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Incorrect.</p>\n<p>Adding another highly correlated asset may provide little diversification benefit.</p>\n<p>If all of your holdings are extremely sensitive to the same economic risk factors, merely increasing the number of securities does not solve your concentration problem.</p>\n<p>The relevant question is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Does the new asset reduce portfolio concentration or covariance?}}$$</div>\n<p>Not:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>How many stocks do I now own?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>That is why correlation is central to Chapter 5.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5n-s35\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">35. Evaluate: \"A stock with beta 0.5 is safer than a stock with beta 1.5\"</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>This statement needs qualification.</p>\n<p>The beta 0.5 stock has lower market sensitivity.</p>\n<p>However, it may still have substantial company-specific risk.</p>\n<p>Suppose:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Stock A</th>\n<th>Stock B</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Beta</td>\n<td>0.5</td>\n<td>1.5</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Total standard deviation</td>\n<td>45%</td>\n<td>30%</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>A has lower beta, but greater total volatility.</p>\n<p>Therefore:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Stock A has lower systematic market exposure, but it cannot be declared unconditionally safer from beta alone.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>This is a stronger investment answer than simply saying lower beta means lower risk.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5n-s36\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">36. Evaluate: \"An investor should buy the highest-beta stock to earn the highest return\"</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>False.</p>\n<p>CAPM relates beta to required expected return.</p>\n<p>It does not guarantee realized returns.</p>\n<p>A high-beta stock might have:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\beta=2.0$$</div>\n<p>and still generate:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$-40\\%$$</div>\n<p>over a particular year.</p>\n<p>More market sensitivity can magnify losses during adverse market conditions.</p>\n<p>A proper evaluation compares the security's expected return with its required return while considering the investor's portfolio exposures.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5n-s37\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">37. Evaluate: \"A portfolio below the efficient frontier should be selected because it feels safer\"</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>This confuses psychological comfort with portfolio efficiency.</p>\n<p>If another attainable portfolio has the same risk but a higher expected return, the original portfolio is dominated under MPT's assumptions.</p>\n<p>Why accept an inferior tradeoff?</p>\n<p>A risk-averse investor does not need to choose an inefficient portfolio.</p>\n<p>They can choose a lower-risk point on the efficient frontier.</p>\n<p>This distinction is useful for higher-order exam questions involving risk tolerance.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5n-s38\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">38. Evaluate: \"Diversification eliminates risk\"</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Incorrect.</p>\n<p>Diversification primarily reduces unsystematic risk.</p>\n<p>It does not necessarily eliminate systematic risk arising from broad market movements.</p>\n<p>Moreover, the effectiveness of diversification depends on asset weights and correlations.</p>\n<p>In a major market downturn, correlations among risky assets may increase.</p>\n<p>Therefore:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{ \\text{Diversification improves risk management but does not guarantee loss prevention.} }$$</div></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5n-s39\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">39. A complete integrated investment case</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Suppose you're evaluating three portfolio strategies.</p>\n<p>Assume the following illustrative estimates:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Portfolio</th>\n<th>Expected return</th>\n<th>Standard deviation</th>\n<th>Beta</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A</td>\n<td>9%</td>\n<td>12%</td>\n<td>0.7</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B</td>\n<td>12%</td>\n<td>18%</td>\n<td>1.1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>C</td>\n<td>14%</td>\n<td>28%</td>\n<td>1.5</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Assume:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R_f=4\\%$$</div>\n<p>and:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$E(R_m)=10\\%$$</div>\n<p>First, calculate CAPM-required returns.</p>\n<p>For A:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R_A=4\\%+0.7(6\\%)$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{R_A=8.2\\%}$$</div>\n<p>For B:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R_B=4\\%+1.1(6\\%)$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{R_B=10.6\\%}$$</div>\n<p>For C:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R_C=4\\%+1.5(6\\%)$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{R_C=13\\%}$$</div>\n<p>Now compare expected and required returns.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Portfolio</th>\n<th>Expected</th>\n<th>CAPM Required</th>\n<th>Expected alpha</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A</td>\n<td>9%</td>\n<td>8.2%</td>\n<td>+0.8%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B</td>\n<td>12%</td>\n<td>10.6%</td>\n<td>+1.4%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>C</td>\n<td>14%</td>\n<td>13.0%</td>\n<td>+1.0%</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>All three have positive expected alpha under these assumptions.</p>\n<p>But that does not automatically make C the best.</p>\n<p>Portfolio C also has the greatest total volatility.</p>\n<p>Portfolio A has the smallest standard deviation and beta.</p>\n<p>Portfolio B has the highest estimated alpha.</p>\n<p>A defensible choice therefore depends on:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>investor risk tolerance;</li>\n<li>confidence in the expected-return estimates;</li>\n<li>whether the options are feasible and appropriately diversified;</li>\n<li>total risk, systematic risk and other investment constraints.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This is how Chapter 4 and Chapter 5 work together.</p>\n<p>Chapter 4 teaches us to estimate and evaluate return and risk.</p>\n<p>Chapter 5 teaches us to evaluate those characteristics for the entire portfolio, not merely individual securities.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5n-s40\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">40. Bloom's Taxonomy: what you should actually be capable of doing</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The chapter becomes much easier to master if you know what depth of understanding is required.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Bloom level</th>\n<th>What mastery looks like</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Remember</td>\n<td>Define portfolio, correlation, beta, systematic risk, CAPM and efficient frontier</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Understand</td>\n<td>Explain why correlation affects portfolio risk and why only systematic risk is priced under CAPM</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Apply</td>\n<td>Calculate portfolio return, standard deviation, beta and CAPM-required return</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Analyse</td>\n<td>Decompose total risk, compare portfolios and identify the effects of correlations</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Evaluate</td>\n<td>Judge whether a portfolio is efficient, properly diversified and adequately compensated for risk</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Create</td>\n<td>Design a portfolio allocation and justify its weights, expected return, risk and systematic exposure</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The higher levels become particularly important in case-based questions where several formulas and concepts must be combined.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5n-s41\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">41. Difficult exam-style questions</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>These are the types of questions I would use to test genuine understanding.</p>\n<p><strong>Q1. Two investments each have a standard deviation of 15%. Could an equally weighted portfolio of them have a standard deviation below 15%?</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> If their correlation is less than +1, the portfolio's standard deviation can be lower than 15%. Correlation determines the diversification benefit.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Q2. Why does CAPM use beta instead of standard deviation to determine required return?</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> CAPM assumes investors can diversify away firm-specific risk. Therefore, systematic market risk, measured by beta, is the relevant risk for determining the equilibrium required return.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Q3. An investor holds 40 stocks, all in the same banking industry. Is the portfolio necessarily well diversified?</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> No. The portfolio may reduce some individual-bank risk, but can remain highly exposed to common industry and economic shocks.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Q4. Portfolio A has beta 0.7 and standard deviation 30%. Portfolio B has beta 1.2 and standard deviation 20%. Which is riskier?</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> A has greater total volatility, while B has greater market sensitivity. Neither is universally riskier without specifying the relevant risk measure and investor objective.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Q5. If two feasible portfolios have identical standard deviations, but one has higher expected return, which is efficient?</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> The higher-return portfolio dominates the lower-return portfolio, assuming the specified risk and return dimensions capture the relevant comparison.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Q6. Why can an investor rationally include an asset with a low individual expected return?</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> It may exhibit low or negative covariance with existing holdings and thereby improve the overall portfolio's risk-return characteristics. Individual expected return is not the only relevant contribution.</p>\n</blockquote></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5n-s42\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">42. The formula sheet you should know</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Concept</th>\n<th>Formula</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Portfolio expected return</td>\n<td>$E(R_p)=\\sum w_iE(R_i)$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Two-asset portfolio variance</td>\n<td>$\\sigma_p^2=w_A^2\\sigma_A^2+w_B^2\\sigma_B^2+2w_Aw_B\\sigma_A\\sigma_B\\rho_{AB}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Portfolio standard deviation</td>\n<td>$\\sigma_p=\\sqrt{\\sigma_p^2}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Correlation</td>\n<td>$\\rho_{AB}=\\frac{\\operatorname{Cov}(R_A,R_B)}{\\sigma_A\\sigma_B}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Individual beta</td>\n<td>$\\beta_i=\\frac{\\operatorname{Cov}(R_i,R_m)}{\\operatorname{Var}(R_m)}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Portfolio beta</td>\n<td>$\\beta_p=\\sum w_i\\beta_i$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Market risk premium</td>\n<td>$E(R_m)-R_f$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>CAPM-required return</td>\n<td>$R_i=R_f+\\beta_i(E(R_m)-R_f)$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Expected CAPM alpha</td>\n<td>$\\alpha_i=E(R_i)-R_{i,\\text{CAPM}}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The technical challenge is not merely remembering these formulas.</p>\n<p>It is knowing which economic question each formula answers.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5n-s43\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">43. The five concepts I would prioritize hardest for the exam</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><ol>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Correlation and diversification:</strong> Understand mathematically why combining assets with correlation below +1 can reduce portfolio risk.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Systematic versus unsystematic risk:</strong> Understand which risks can be diversified away and why systematic risk matters for CAPM.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Beta versus standard deviation:</strong> Standard deviation measures total volatility; beta measures market sensitivity.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>CAPM and the SML:</strong> Derive required returns, calculate market risk premiums and evaluate whether expected returns justify beta.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Efficient frontier:</strong> Understand portfolio dominance and why optimal allocation depends on investor objectives and risk preferences.</p>\n</li>\n</ol></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5n-s44\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">44. The deepest lesson of Chapter 5</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>In Chapter 4, the basic question was:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{What return does this individual investment provide for its risk?}}$$</div>\n<p>Chapter 5 fundamentally changes the perspective:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{How does this investment change the risk and return of my entire portfolio?}}$$</div>\n<p>That is why a risky stock can still have a valuable role in a portfolio.</p>\n<p>That is why correlation is often more important than simply counting investments.</p>\n<p>That is why beta and standard deviation measure different dimensions of risk.</p>\n<p>And that is why the most attractive portfolio is not necessarily the one made up of the individually highest-return assets.</p>\n<p>The chapter concludes by recommending a combination of diversification across industries and securities, correlation analysis, portfolio beta management and evaluating alternative portfolios against the investor's acceptable risk level.</p>\n<p>The entire chapter can ultimately be summarized as:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{ \\text{Portfolio Quality} \\neq \\text{Sum of Individual Asset Quality} }$$</div>\n<p>Instead:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{ \\text{Portfolio Quality} = f(\\text{Returns, Weights, Risks, Correlations, Objectives}) }$$</div>\n<p>A superior investor does not merely identify good investments. They understand how investments interact, and construct a portfolio whose overall behavior is appropriate for the risk they are prepared to bear.</p></div></details></div></details>");
